<ProseLayout
  title="thingGLANCE Privacy Policy"
  description="How thingGLANCE handles your data. Your things, plans and dates stay on your device: no accounts, no analytics, and nothing sent to us."
  lastUpdated="November 15, 2026"
>
  <p class="lead">
    thingGLANCE keeps track of the dates attached to the things you own:
    warranties, subscriptions, support windows and planned replacements.
    Everything you enter (your things, their coverage, your plans, what they
    cost, and the names of the people in your household) is stored on your
    device. This policy explains what thingGLANCE accesses, where your data can
    go, and who controls it.
  </p>

  <ControllerIdentity>
    <p>
      This policy covers thingGLANCE specifically: the Android, iPhone and iPad
      apps (com.thingglance.app), the desktop apps for macOS, Windows and Linux,
      and the web app. For our publisher-level practices and the
      glance-apps.com website, see the{' '}
      <a href="/privacy">GLANCE Apps privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </ControllerIdentity>

  <h2>Data we do not collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not collect personal information.</li>
    <li>We do not use analytics, advertising, tracking or crash reporting.</li>
    <li>We do not have user accounts.</li>
    <li>
      We cannot read anything you sync. It is encrypted on your device before
      it leaves, with a key made from a passphrase only you know.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    thingGLANCE does not ask for your location, contacts, camera, photos or
    microphone. It reads or writes a file only when you export or import a
    backup yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Data the app accesses</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing below reaches GLANCE Apps. Sync, the connections to other apps and
    automation are off until you turn them on. The web lookup is on, and is
    used only when you use it.
  </p>

  <h3>Web lookup</h3>
  <p>
    When you add a thing, you can type its name into the lookup box to have
    thingGLANCE fill in a photo, specifications, a release date and the date
    its security updates end. To do that, the name you type is sent to
    Wikipedia, and thingGLANCE asks endoflife.date about the product it
    matches. Nothing else is sent with it: not your other things, and nothing
    that identifies you beyond what any request on the internet carries, such
    as your IP address.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It happens only when you type into the lookup box. Adding a thing by
      hand sends nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      A photo found this way is not copied into the app. It is loaded from
      Wikimedia's servers when the thing is shown.
    </li>
    <li>
      A switch in Settings turns the web lookup off altogether.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Wikipedia is run by the Wikimedia Foundation, and endoflife.date is an
    independent public project. Their own privacy policies govern what they do
    with a request.
  </p>

  <h3>Sync</h3>
  <p>
    thingGLANCE syncs only through GLANCEvault, and only if you connect it to
    one. Sync is off until you do.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your data is always encrypted on your device before it is sent, with a
      key made from a sync passphrase you choose. The server stores encrypted
      content only. It never has your things, plans, dates, amounts, household
      names or passphrase in a form it can read.
    </li>
    <li>
      To route and merge changes, the server does see an opaque identifier for
      each item and when it was last changed, along with the account and device
      credentials you connected with.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your passphrase is never stored. The key made from it is kept on your
      device in its secure storage (the Android Keystore, the iOS Keychain,
      the macOS Keychain, Windows' protected storage, or the Linux keyring) so
      you are not asked for it on every launch. Where no secure storage is
      available (the web app, or Linux without a keyring), it is kept in the
      app's local storage. If you forget your passphrase, synced data cannot
      be recovered.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Household: the names of your household's members are synced too, encrypted
    in the same way, so that your other devices and other GLANCE Apps on the
    same vault recognize the same people. An item you mark private is hidden on
    other members' devices; it is still synced, encrypted, to the vault your
    household shares.
  </p>

  <h3>Connections to other GLANCE Apps and automation</h3>
  <p>
    These send nothing to GLANCE Apps.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      dayGLANCE (off by default): thingGLANCE can send a date to dayGLANCE as a
      task, and clear the date when the task is completed there. These
      messages use the GLANCEintents protocol, travel through your vault, and
      are always encrypted with your sync passphrase.
    </li>
    <li>
      GLANCElauncher (Android): if GLANCElauncher is installed, it can show
      your dates, things and plan charges on your home screen and pass back
      what you do there, such as clearing a date. This goes directly between
      the two apps on your phone, through an Android permission that only the
      genuine GLANCElauncher holds. See the{' '}
      <a href="/glancelauncher/privacy">GLANCElauncher privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Automation apps such as Tasker (Android, off by default): other apps on
      your phone can ask thingGLANCE to add a thing, a plan or coverage, clear
      a date or open a screen, and can read a summary of what is coming up.
      While this is on, any app installed on your phone can use it. Nothing
      leaves your phone through it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sharing into thingGLANCE (Android, iPhone and iPad): text or a link you
      share to thingGLANCE from another app, such as a product's page, is used
      to name a new thing and goes nowhere else. Nothing is added until you
      save it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Siri, Spotlight and Shortcuts (iPhone and iPad): thingGLANCE offers a
      few shortcuts, such as asking what is next or settling a date. They are
      answered on your device from the summary the app saved there. What
      thingGLANCE hands to iOS is the answer shown or spoken to you and, when
      a shortcut asks which date you mean, the names of the dates waiting. If
      you ask through Siri, Apple handles your request under its own privacy
      policy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Local AI assistants (MCP server)</h3>
  <p>
    The thingGLANCE desktop app (macOS, Windows and Linux) includes an optional
    local server built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP), an open standard
    that lets AI assistant applications on the same computer (such as Claude
    Code) read your thingGLANCE data and, if you allow it, make changes. It is
    off by default. In Settings, then Integrations, then On this computer, you
    turn on reading and, separately, changes. thingGLANCE shows you an
    explanation and asks you to confirm before each of these takes effect. The
    server listens only on your own computer (127.0.0.1) and is never reachable
    from the network. Applications connect with an access token that
    thingGLANCE generates; you can replace it at any time, which disconnects
    every application that had the old one.
  </p>
  <p>
    With reading on, an application you connect can read your things and their
    details, notes and coverage, your plans and what they cost, and the dates
    ahead and those waiting for a decision. If you share thingGLANCE with your
    household, it can also read the names of the other members and see what
    each owns, pays for or deals with. It reads as the member the computer
    belongs to: another member's private items are not shown to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    With changes allowed, a connected application can add things, coverage and
    plans, edit things and plans, change whose a thing is, and settle a date in
    your queue. It can never delete anything. It can make at most 30 changes a
    minute, and the changes it has made since thingGLANCE last started are
    listed in Settings, where you can undo them.
  </p>
  <p>
    While the server is on, the version downloaded from GitHub keeps its port
    and access token in a file in its own data folder on your computer, so a
    connected application can find it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Assistant applications typically transmit what they read to third-party AI
    providers over the internet. thingGLANCE itself transmits nothing. The
    connection is local, and any data that leaves your device is sent by the
    client application you chose, installed, and configured, not by
    thingGLANCE. thingGLANCE has no relationship with, and no visibility into,
    those providers, and cannot see, limit, or audit what a connected client
    does with data it has read. This is outside the scope of thingGLANCE's own
    encryption and no-server-access guarantees, which cover how thingGLANCE
    stores and syncs your data, not what an application you authorized does
    after reading it. You are responsible for reviewing the privacy policy of
    any client you connect (and the AI provider behind it).
  </p>

  <h3>Stream Deck</h3>
  <p>
    The thingGLANCE desktop app can work with an Elgato Stream Deck through the
    thingGLANCE Stream Deck plugin. This is off by default; you turn it on in
    Settings, then Integrations, then On this computer. While it is on,
    thingGLANCE listens only on your own computer (127.0.0.1) and gives the
    plugin what its keys show: the dates waiting for a decision and those
    coming up, what your plans will charge over the next 30 days, and your
    things' names and how long each has left. From a key, the plugin can mark
    a date in your queue done and open thingGLANCE at a date, a thing, your
    plans or the new-thing form. The plugin runs inside Elgato's Stream Deck
    software on your computer, and neither thingGLANCE nor the plugin sends
    this data over the internet. As with any local connection, other software
    running on your computer could also connect while it is on. Elgato's
    privacy policy governs the Stream Deck software.
  </p>

  <h3>Notifications, widgets and other surfaces</h3>
  <p>
    Reminders are scheduled on your device. Widgets (on the Home Screen and,
    on iPhone and iPad, the Lock Screen), Android's Quick Settings tiles and
    the desktop app's tray read a summary of your dates, things and plan
    charges that the app saves on your device. On Android a background refresh
    keeps that summary current without the app being opened. None of this is
    sent anywhere, except that a refresh also syncs with your vault if you
    have connected one.
  </p>

  <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Your data is kept in the app's private storage. On Android, thingGLANCE
    keeps it out of your platform's automatic backup (Google Backup): moving to
    a new phone is done through your vault, or with a backup file you export
    from Settings and import again. Nothing is exported unless you do it.
    Uninstalling thingGLANCE deletes everything it stored on your phone.
  </p>
  <p>
    On iPhone and iPad, your data is part of your device's own backup (iCloud
    Backup, or a backup to your computer) if you use one, under Apple's terms.
    Your vault connection and encryption key are not: they are kept in the
    Keychain on that device only. Deleting thingGLANCE deletes everything else
    it stored on the device; iOS can keep Keychain entries after an app is
    deleted, and thingGLANCE clears any it finds when it is installed again.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a computer, your data is kept in thingGLANCE's data folder in your user
    account. Removing the app can leave that folder behind; deleting the
    folder removes the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    One small record is the exception. A copy of thingGLANCE installed from
    Google Play notes that it was bought: the store's name, the date Android
    first installed the app, and the app's version. It holds nothing about you
    or your payment. It is left in your own device backup, and synced to your
    vault if you use one, so that it follows you to a new phone. The app does
    not use it to allow or refuse anything. A copy installed from the App
    Store or the Mac App Store notes the same three things, with the date the
    store gives for the purchase (on a Mac, the day the app first ran there).
    The desktop apps downloaded from GitHub and the web app record nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the web app, your data is kept in your browser's storage on that
    device. Clearing your browser's site data removes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <p>
    thingGLANCE does not integrate with any third-party analytics, advertising,
    or data brokers. The only third-party services involved are those you
    choose to use:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Wikipedia and Wikimedia (web lookup, and the photos it finds)</li>
    <li>endoflife.date (web lookup)</li>
    <li>The GLANCEvault server you connect to, if you turn on sync</li>
    <li>
      Google Play or the Apple App Store (the purchase, on the versions from
      those stores only)
    </li>
    <li>
      Any MCP client application you connect, only if you enable the optional
      MCP server in the desktop app: the client reads thingGLANCE data locally
      and may send it to its own AI provider
    </li>
    <li>
      Elgato Stream Deck software, only if you use the thingGLANCE Stream Deck
      plugin
    </li>
    <li>
      GitHub (api.github.com): the desktop app downloaded from GitHub checks
      for new releases; no personal data is sent
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Purchases</h2>
  <p>
    The versions on Google Play, the App Store and the Mac App Store are paid
    apps, bought once. The purchase is processed entirely by Google Play or
    by the Apple App Store. We do not see or store your payment information,
    and the app has no subscriptions or in-app purchases. The desktop apps
    downloaded from GitHub and the web app are free. The applicable store's
    privacy policy governs payment processing.
  </p>

  <ChildrensPrivacy />

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes materially, we will update the "Last updated" date
    above and note the change in the app's release notes.
  </p>

  <PrivacyContact />
</ProseLayout>
